+/// Format-on-enter: the whole program a completed buffer holds is normalised
+/// by the formatter before it runs.
+///
+/// A block typed on one line — `if x is 3 then say "a" end` — is the shape a
+/// user types and not the shape the formatter writes, so everything a session
+/// then wrote down, exported, or reloaded was in the shape nobody would. The
+/// block is normalised at the moment the buffer balances rather than line by
+/// line, so continuation mode is untouched: only a complete program is ever
+/// rewritten, and the lines that went into it are still the ones on screen.
+///
+/// A program the formatter refuses is run exactly as it was typed. Formatting
+/// is a convenience here, not a gate: replacing source the formatter cannot
+/// read would turn a bad indentation into a line that does not run.
+fn format_on_enter(code: &str) -> String {
+    match crate::formatter::format(code) {
+        Ok(formatted) if formatted.trim_end() != code.trim_end() => formatted,
+        _ => code.to_string(),
+    }
+}
